<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallia Narbonensis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 121 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Provincia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gallia</a> Narbonensis was the Roman province of southern Gaul between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, formed after Roman campaigns against the Allobroges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> around 121 BCE and named after its capital, Narbo. The Gallic peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and the Greek city of Massalia came under it. Its name survives in the region of Provence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis.jpg" alt="Gallia Narbonensis" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Narbonensis_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gallia Narbonensis lay in what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occitania">Occitania</a> and Provence in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It was bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> on the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cevennes">Cévennes</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> on the east and the Gulf of Lion on the south, and it included most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> catchment. Its western part was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called it Provincia Nostra, 'Our Province', or simply Provincia, because it was the first Roman province north of the Alps. It was also known as Gallia Transalpina, to distinguish it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The name Gallia Narbonensis most likely arose in the Augustan era, and its first recorded use was in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> conducted by Gnaeus Pullius Pollio.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Greek colony of Massalia was founded about 600 BCE, and with it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenization">Hellenisation</a> of Celtic Gaul began. Massalia, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Massilia, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which agreed to protect it in exchange for information and a fleet. Powerful Gaulish tribes, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> and the Allobroges, threatened the coastal settlements. In the First Transalpine War (125–121 BCE), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a> defeated the Allobroges and the Arverni under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a> at the Battle of the Isère River, and the area became a Roman province in 121 BCE.</p><p>In 118 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbo Martius</a>, modern Narbonne, and built the Via Domitia, the first Roman road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, linking Gaul to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. The Via Aquitania led toward the Atlantic through Tolosa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a> (Bordeaux). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> rebuilt Narbo, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frejus">Fréjus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, and granted citizenship to many communities. Massilia sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> in 49 BCE and afterwards lost its independence. Augustus made the province a senatorial one governed by a proconsul.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reorganisation merged Gallia Narbonensis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Gallia Aquitania</a> into the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, with its capital at Vienne. The province and its surroundings were incorporated into the Visigothic Kingdom between 462 and 477, which ended Roman political control. The Visigothic lands became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a>, while to the east of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> the name Provence came into use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gallia Narbonensis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Roman Republic destroyed a Gallic army at Telamon in 225 BCE, conquered Cisalpine Gaul by the early 2nd century BCE, fought the Celtiberians until the fall of Numantia in 133 BCE and made southern Gaul the province of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b> about 121 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Their land was then absorbed into <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/albici">Albici</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome defeated them in 121 BCE, and their land became part of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/allobroges">Allobroges</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory later lay within the Roman province of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/deciates">Deciates</a></li><li><blockquote>In 118 BCE Rome founded the colony of Narbo Martius next to Montlaurès, which became the capital of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/elisyces">Elisyces</a></li><li><blockquote>A steady ally of the Roman Republic, Massalia called in Roman armies against the Salyes from 125 BCE, opening the way to the province of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a></li></ul></section>
